      "narrative": "Mara's eyebrow cocks at your question. A thin smile crosses her lips \u2014 not friendly, not hostile. Measuring.\n\n\"Lockwright work, is it?\" She taps the rim of her cup. \"You're fishing, ranger. I can see the hook.\"\n\nShe pauses, letting the silence stretch. Toran Vex shuffles his papers, the rustle loud in the quiet.\n\nBut the answer doesn't come.\n\nInstead, Mara picks up her tea, takes a slow sip, and sets it down again. Her eyes never leave yours.\n\n\"The man who kept that lock is dead,\" she says flatly. \"Old Bram. Ferried folks through the gate before the seal. He knew every tooth of that mechanism.\"\n\nShe lets that land. Then she adds, almost offhand:\n\n\"Funny thing, though. Bram's hands were too gnarled to file a lock clean. He was a ferryman, not a smith. So if someone filed that gate shut... it wasn't him.\"\n\nShe watches you closely, waiting to see if the name means anything.\n\nToran Vex clears his throat softly, his pen frozen over the page."
    },
