      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the dim warmth of copper lamps and sour ale. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp, carrying the iron tang of wet stone and something older, something that clings to the Ash Gate like a held breath.\n\nYou cross the short span of cobbled ground between the tavern and the gate. It rises before you: a cracked black arch, fifteen feet high, set into no wall \u2014 standing alone against the grey sky like a broken tooth. The stone is dark, veined with pale scars where weather and time have gnawed at it.\n\nThe gate itself is a slab of rusted iron, fitted into the arch. A heavy lock hangs from a hasp \u2014 and you can see it clearly now. The keyhole is scored, the edges bright and raw where metal was filed away. Someone worked this lock with deliberate care, not brute force. A fine powder of rust and iron dust still clings to the lower edge of the hasp.\n\nThe ground before the gate is scuffed \u2014 boot prints, old ones, overlapping. A few scraps of dried moss. And against the base of the arch, half-hidden in shadow, a dark stain spreads across the stone.\n\nThe air is still. The gate is silent.\n\nA crow lands on the top curve of the arch, cocks its head, and watches you."
    },
